# What must never enter the build context. Two reasons for anything here:
# it would leak, or it would be copied only to be thrown away.

# Secrets. `.env.local` holds live keys and, since standalone does not read
# .env files anyway, shipping it would be pure risk for zero benefit — every
# runtime value arrives as a real process env var from a Fly secret.
.env
.env.*

# No `!.env.example` exemption. It was here so the template travelled with the
# context, but nothing in the build reads it — Next loads .env, .env.local and
# .env.<NODE_ENV>, never .env.example — so its only effect was to punch a hole
# in the `.env.*` rule and leave a file named like a secret sitting in an image
# layer, one careless `COPY` away from the runtime stage. It stays in git,
# which is where a template belongs. Verified: the build succeeds without it.

# Installed and built locally on a developer's machine, with that machine's
# libc and that machine's env baked in. The image builds both itself.
node_modules
.next
out
build
coverage

.git
.gitignore
.vercel
*.pem
*.tsbuildinfo
.DS_Store
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# Deploy definitions describe the image; they are not part of it.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
fly.toml
.github

# Docs, database migrations and one-off scripts. None of them are imported by
# anything `next build` compiles, so they would only pad the context.
*.md
supabase
scripts
